FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2401-2414 — Member completion only recognises an object or a **stdlib** module; `name_of(receiver)`, `is_module(module)`, and the `MODULE_MEMBERS` lookup have no path for a program-declared `module … end` or a file-imported module. `import MathUtils` / `module text … end` therefore makes `MathUtils.` (the canonical example in AGENTS.md) return `None` from `member_completions` and `Ok(vec![])` from `completions`, even though `interpreter::module_member_values` can enumerate those members. The report's "Known gaps" only mentions interpolation, not this. — Fix: record declared-module members during the walk (analogous to `object_methods`) and consult that table before bailing; add a `module text … end` / file-import completion test. — Traced: `module_namespace` is true only when `is_module(module)` is true (std::stdlib `MODULES`), and the fallback arms at 2410-2414 return an empty slice for every other module.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2395-2403 — `module_aliases` is one flat `HashMap<String, String>` (declared at src/lsp.rs:1240, filled at 1663/1689) with no scope information, but `member_completions` consults it whenever `token_binding[receiver]` is `None`. An alias declared in a function/branch (`to f()\n    import files as g\nend\ng.`) keeps `g → files` after the block, so completion after the out-of-scope `g.` offers `files` members even though the analyzer reports `Unknown variable 'g'`. This contradicts the phase's own `edge_nested_import_stays_in_its_block` scoping claim for navigation. — Fix: store the alias on the import binding/scope (or require an in-scope `DefinitionKind::Import` binding before consulting the alias map), and add a test for an alias used after its enclosing block ends. — Traced: the use outside the function has `token_binding[receiver] == None`, and the `None => is_module(module)` arm resolves the leaked alias.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
